      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "### Environment\n\nNode version: v22.19.0\nnpm version: v10.5.0\nLocal ESLint version: v10.9.0\nGlobal ESLint version: Not found\nOperating System: win32 10.0.26200\n\n\n### What parser are you using?\n\nDefault (Espree)\n\n### What did you do?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Configuration</summary>\n\n```js\nexport default [\n\t{\n\t\tlanguageOptions: {\n\t\t\tsourceType: \"script\"\n\t\t},\n\t\trules: {\n\t\t\t\"prefer-object-has-own\": \"error\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n];\n```\n</details>\n\n```js\nlet Object = {\n\tprototype: {\n\t\thasOwnProperty: {\n\t\t\tcall() {}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n};\n\nObject.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call({}, \"foo\");\n```\n\n\n### What did you expect to happen?\n\nNo error should be reported and no autofix should be applied, because `Object` here refers to a user-defined global variable, not the built-in `Object` constructor.\n\n\n### What actually happened?\n\n```\nUse 'Object.hasOwn()' instead of 'Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call()'.\n```\n\nWith `--fix`, the last line is rewritten to\n\n```js\nObject.hasOwn({}, \"foo\");\n```\n\n\n### Link to Minimal Reproducible Example\n\nhttps://eslint.org/play/#\n\n### Participation\n\n- [x] I am willing to submit a pull request for this issue.\n\n### AI acknowledgment\n\n- [x] I did not use AI to generate this issue report.\n- [ ] (If the above is not checked) I have reviewed the AI-generated content before submitting.\n\n### Additional comments\n\nDisclosure: I'm a participant of [open source contribution program OSSCA](https://github.com/eslint-ossca)",
